---
run: round-trip
fixpoint: executed
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-readonly-set-in-sourced-file — QUESTION: a sourced file marks a variable `readonly`.
# Does the attribute reach the caller, and what does the caller's later assignment to it do — fail
# quietly, or end the script?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it, and why it is its own case rather than a cell of
# `floor30-atlas-sourced-file-state-persists`: assigning to a readonly variable is a
# variable-assignment error, which POSIX allows a non-interactive shell to EXIT on. A dependency
# that freezes a name an admin's book later writes would end the run at a line the admin wrote in
# good faith.
#
# ANSWERED, and the shells AGREE on everything that answers the question — they differ only in the
# status of a construct they both rejected, which `fence-rejection-rc` says no dialect rule may
# depend on. Gate-9 cannot tell that apart from a real divergence, so this case carries NO
# `expected.emitted` section; re-arming it is one line, and would go red on a status nothing is
# entitled to read.
#
# measured-divergence: (mise run test:floor -- floor30-atlas, dash 0.5.12 ∩ posh 0.14.1, WSL)
#   dash: file-freezing
#         file-frozen
#         book: after-dot value=[start]
#         book: contained status=[2]
#         book-between
#   posh: file-freezing
#         file-frozen
#         book: after-dot value=[start]
#         book: contained status=[1]
#         book-between
#   MEANING: the ONLY differing byte is the rejected assignment's status (2 vs 1). Both shells agree
#   that the `readonly` attribute REACHES the caller, that the value stays `start`, that an
#   assignment to it ends the enclosing shell — the subshell cell dies and is caught, the bare cell
#   takes the script with it, which is why `book: bare-assign-survived` and `book-tail` appear in
#   neither. So the mechanic is settled and the divergence is `fence-rejection-rc` material.
ro_probe=start
. ./freezer.sh
printf 'book: after-dot value=[%s]\n' "$ro_probe"
( ro_probe=changed-in-sub; printf 'sub: assign-survived value=[%s]\n' "$ro_probe" ) || printf 'book: contained status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'book-between\n'
ro_probe=changed
printf 'book: bare-assign-survived value=[%s]\n' "$ro_probe"
printf 'book-tail\n'

-- freezer.sh --
printf 'file-freezing\n'
readonly ro_probe
printf 'file-frozen\n'

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=6a56e0e3b93b28f6ad053c49c2ea1621411e8aad66954301dbfea905d075ff2f sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:6 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:7 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:8 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:9 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

